{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790254661,"nonce":"+o9WerJb+u7AF2TsrJOcyQ3WC+E=","title":"Python: type() of a typed array prints list, and a boxed array has no typecode","message":"Two gaps in how an array.array reads once its static type is lost or asked for.\n\n    from array import array\n    a = array('B', [1])\n    print(type(a))            # python3: \u003cclass 'array.array'\u003e; zypy: list\n\n    def middle(seq):\n        return seq[1:3]\n    print(middle([1, 2, 3]))\n    s = middle(array('B', [9, 8, 7, 6]))\n    print(s.typecode)         # python3: B; zypy: AttributeError: 'list' object has no attribute 'typecode'\n\nBoth reproduce at dea6d08b (~/bin-main/zypy on the NUC). The first is the\ntyped type() of List(Elem::Array(_)) naming the storage; the second is\nattribute access on a boxed array (custom kind at or above\nARRAY_KIND_BASE) not reaching typecode/itemsize, which the kind already\nencodes (letter in the bits above the storage kind).","files":null}]}